#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "01993d51-8a9f-5156-b5cc-2784e93da5aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5753" :question/problem "Determine if $x+3$ is a factor of $p(x)=x^3+3x^2-3x-9.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(x-r)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = -3$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo, we just need to check if $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x).$ In other words, we need to check if $p(-3)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-3(-3)-9 \\\\  & =-27+27+9-9 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, $p(-3)=0,$ so $x = -3$ is a root of the polynomial. Consequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $p(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5d3a7d-ef95-5d7d-a92a-ce76d6b400bb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem to Factors With Leading Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "da4e2c42-894b-5f2a-917b-a5df22f41019" :question/math-academy-id "e-5754" :question/problem "Determine if $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)=-6x^2+11x-4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(ax-b)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = \\dfrac{b}{a}$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(4-3x).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3x & =0 \\\\ 4 & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is also a root of $p(x).$\n\nSo, we just need to check if $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of $p(x).$ In other words, we need to check if $p\\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) =0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\frac{4}{3}) & =-6{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+11(\\frac{4}{3})-4 \\\\  & =-6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{44}{3}-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{-32+44-12}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, $p \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)=0,$ so $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of the polynomial. Consequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(4-3x)$ is indeed a factor of $p(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2120844b-39e0-50a0-83b7-91462ed37227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522095} {:answer/id #uuid "87ee6efa-aeba-5dd5-bf88-cb72af28c2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522290} {:answer/id #uuid "917aaf23-2a71-5c96-9781-e7bc343926d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522318} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c1532d-208d-51db-bb26-0e792b6f66e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522363} {:answer/id #uuid "c05ab628-4248-5c64-bd22-1434aff7961e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4c1532d-208d-51db-bb26-0e792b6f66e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522363} :answer-field/id #uuid "410d3b37-dbfa-5b15-aa19-c40da8cea6c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35496066-8708-503f-bd5d-57e7d1387c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-112455" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(x-2)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-10$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{3}+8$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{3}+4x-16$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{2}+3(2)-10 \\\\  & =4+6-10 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{3}+8 \\\\  & =8+8 \\\\  & =16\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{3}+4(2)-16 \\\\  & =8+8-16 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $h(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5d3a7d-ef95-5d7d-a92a-ce76d6b400bb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem to Factors With Leading Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5b5a30-e0e7-5744-b422-7047faa2e338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522040} {:answer/id #uuid "619e746c-8ca9-559a-ba02-5e619348d205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522222} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec0dca9-31ae-5610-aaf5-c02dc410d7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522269} {:answer/id #uuid "962be32f-d335-5bd3-b444-dcb1f56ce30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522327} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c39bb2-9093-5603-adeb-9895d5238cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "619e746c-8ca9-559a-ba02-5e619348d205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522222} :answer-field/id #uuid "789b2306-2fd7-57dc-bf7b-7225dc82a697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e0ff1de-8233-57fb-9fb2-58728b6e261f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112465" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-25?$\n\nI. $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x-5).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{5}{2}) & =4{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-25 \\\\  & =4(\\frac{25}{4})-25 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(2x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(2x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd5d3a7d-ef95-5d7d-a92a-ce76d6b400bb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem to Factors With Leading Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2394e902-5877-5810-8394-10ee68af9948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522099} {:answer/id #uuid "340065a2-9812-543e-996f-7f17091e976f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522136} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0a9111-8e94-5364-bc20-efc06aaf2f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522260} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6d1d0b-251e-586b-a73a-66b5615b60f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522522} {:answer/id #uuid "feea11ca-e2b0-5234-8225-36492bbaedf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2394e902-5877-5810-8394-10ee68af9948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522099} :answer-field/id #uuid "bae3461d-fd61-5c28-bb95-fe22bb6cd076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd794f4b-e202-5786-ad78-bc211e098112" :question/math-academy-id "q-112486" :question/problem "For which of the following polynomials is $(5x+4)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}$\n\nII. $g(x)=15{x}^{2}+25x+4$\n\nIII. $h(x)=4{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(5x+4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(5x+4)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ is a root of $p(x).$\n\nNow we will check if $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{4}{5}) & =5{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}+4{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =5(-\\frac{64}{125})+4(\\frac{16}{25}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{64}{25}+\\frac{64}{25} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(5x+4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-\\frac{4}{5}) & =15{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}+25(-\\frac{4}{5})+4 \\\\  & =15(\\frac{16}{25})-20+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}-16 \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{5}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(5x+4)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-\\frac{4}{5}) & =4{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}+5{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{64}{125})+5(\\frac{16}{25}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{256}{125}+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{125}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(5x+4)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e54810-6a24-5857-8888-1cb2a6673578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522035} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4880bd-d3bf-5682-b1f3-41c300f391e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522043} {:answer/id #uuid "11d140f3-1414-532b-a8c7-d796e0fb4c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522054} {:answer/id #uuid "56772dec-1569-512c-953e-14ff78e36da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522200} {:answer/id #uuid "889ddc5d-2c8b-58e4-bef6-fa1b4225c168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "889ddc5d-2c8b-58e4-bef6-fa1b4225c168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522293} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a98c68f-f943-512d-913b-2ea65a17cea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80ca9b7a-fc1f-5555-a096-84f9c05d5b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-73772" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x-3?$\n\nI. $x=-3$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-3$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}+2(-3)-3 \\\\  & =9-6-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-3$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
